# Fixed 2026-09-30, defect 131's class, lane recovery-b2 (lane recovery-b1's
# report, U3). Symptom: a string or a character that never closed inside a
# call took the rest of its line, closers included, and the lexer named the
# openers they closed never closed: `x = f("abc + )` cost
# `unterminated_string` and `unclosed_bracket` for one mistake, and
# `print(f("a" + "b))` three; the line below was read inside the bracket.
# And the classic `'\'` had a certain fix over the whole rest of its line:
# `--apply` wrote `f('\' + )` as `f('\\'`, the `+ )` gone and a report the
# first run never made in its place. Cause: a literal that never closed ran
# to its line's end and the stack never heard of the closers it took. Now
# they pop what they close, in the lexer and the parser alike
# (`literals.swallow`, `literals.closers_taken`), the line end after the
# literal is a line end at any depth, and `'\'` ends at its third byte, its
# fix over those bytes alone, the rest of its line lexed and read.

function one_closer_taken()
    x = f("abc + )  #~ unterminated_string
    print(x)

function the_closer_alone()
    y = f("abc)  #~ unterminated_string
    print(y)

function two_closers_taken()
    zs = [f("a), 2]  #~ unterminated_string
    print(zs.len())

function a_second_string()
    print(f("a" + "b))  #~ unterminated_string

function no_closer_taken()
    w = f("abc  #~ unterminated_string unclosed_bracket
    print(w)

function a_stray_closer_below()
    v = f("abc)  #~ unterminated_string
    print(1) )  #~ expected_end_of_line

function the_classic_backslash()
    u = g('\' + )  #~ unterminated_char expected_expression
    print(u)

function the_classic_whole()
    print(g('\'))  #~ unterminated_char

function outside_brackets()
    t = "abc  #~ unterminated_string
    print(t)

# 2026-10-02, lane literals (defect 135's L3): the classic `'\'` above no
# longer takes a certain `'\\'`, a backslash and an apostrophe being one
# character from it alike, so both are guesses; no other mistake here has a
# certain fix either, so `check --apply` leaves this case as it is and it
# keeps no `.applied`.
